//! lotus/stereo_expand.rye -- ALES159: the downward expander carried into stereo, the gate's linked DECISION fused with the compressor's linked GAIN -- completing the core stereo dynamics quartet. ALES60 named `expand`:
//! a sample at or above a magnitude threshold passes byte for byte, a sample below it has its DEFICIT (threshold − m) widened by a ratio and the sample rewritten sign(x)-max(0, threshold − deficit-num/den) -- the
//! compressor mirrored under the threshold, deepening the quiet (the space between phrases opened wider, the room tone under a pause lowered), continuous at the threshold, the gate its hardest limit. That is a mono edit.
//! Carried into stereo it must fuse the two linking laws this family has proven: it links the DECISION like the gate (ALES156) AND the GAIN like the compressor (ALES157).
//!
//! Two truths meet. Its decision must be linked, like the gate: the expander pushes down the quiet, and expanding each channel by whether it alone is below threshold would push the quiet right down while the loud left
//! plays -- the image tears, exactly the gate's bug. So a pair is expanded ONLY when both channels are below threshold (key = max(|left|, |right|) < threshold). Its gain must be linked, like the compressor: the expander's
//! gain factor max(0, threshold − deficit-ratio)/magnitude is magnitude-dependent, so expanding each channel by its own deficit gives different gains and shifts the image. So the one gain is computed from the linked key
//! and applied to both.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadThreshold (a threshold outside [1, sample_max]), BadRatio (a ratio below one or with a zero denominator), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any write. Otherwise walk
//! the span one sample-PAIR at a time with key = max(|left|, |right|): key >= threshold leaves the pair playing -- both pass byte for byte (continuous at the threshold). key == 0 leaves the pair silent -- both stay zero
//! (silence stays silence, no divide by zero). 0 < key < threshold expands -- new_key = max(0, threshold − (threshold − key)-num/den), exactly as ALES60 would for the louder channel, then scale BOTH channels by the
//! single fraction new_key/key: the peak-holding (louder-quiet) channel lands on new_key, the quieter channel scaled by the identical fraction. A ratio large enough drives the whole quiet pair to silence -- the gate
//! reached from within.
//!
//! The crux -- five laws. THE LINKED DECISION + GAIN LAW (the stereo crux): a pair is expanded iff BOTH channels are below the threshold, and where expanded both are scaled by the SAME fraction new_key/key -- so the
//! louder-quiet channel equals ALES60's mono expand byte for byte (a left-always-peak master has left' == mono expand(left)), the independent lift genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R
//! ratio -- an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE AT-THRESHOLD LAW: a pair whose linked key is at or above the threshold passes byte for byte -- continuous at the
//! threshold. THE KNOB LAWS: unit ratio the identity everywhere on both, a higher ratio expands at least as hard (monotone), a ratio far past the threshold drives a quiet pair exactly to silence (the gate within). THE
//! SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a both-silent pair stays silent (the key = 0 guard), left.len == right.len after, a refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES159.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-063651_fill-ales159-lotus-stereo-expand.md
//! Stands on: lotus/expand.rye (ALES60) -- the mono downward expander, its `expand` and `ExpandError` reused whole (the louder-quiet-channel proof and the fault set); lotus/stereo_gate.rye (ALES156) -- the linked-DECISION
//! law this fuses; lotus/stereo_compress.rye (ALES157) -- the linked-GAIN law this fuses; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, buf/len/samples, sample_max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only -- one comparison of the linked key and at most one multiply-then-
//! divide per channel per sample (in i64, |sample|-new_key <= 32768-32768 never overflowing), memoryless (no attack, hold, or release; those are later rungs), never a length. The threshold is a magnitude in sample units,
//! not decibels; the ratio a plain fraction. Nothing on the audio path can overflow -- the widened result is floored at zero and new_key <= key, so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's, which already fits i16. No
//! real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_expand.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const expand = @import("expand.rye");

pub const stereo_expand_version = "20260815.063651";

/// Stereo expansion's faults are exactly ALES60's own `ExpandError` -- `BadThreshold` (a threshold outside [1, sample_max]), `BadRatio` (a ratio below one or with a zero denominator), `BadRange` (a span past the samples) --
/// reused by name, since the linked lift adds no new fault: it validates one shared threshold, one shared ratio, and one span, then decides and scales per pair from both channels together.
pub const StereoExpandError = expand.ExpandError;

/// Expand `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc`, below the magnitude `threshold` by the ratio `ratio_num/ratio_den` (at least one), fusing a LINKED DECISION with a LINKED GAIN. For each index
/// the detector key is `max(|left|, |right|)`: at or above the threshold the pair passes byte for byte (continuous at the threshold); a both-silent pair (key == 0) stays zero; otherwise where the pair is below the
/// threshold the widened master `new_key = max(0, threshold − (threshold − key)-num/den)` is computed once (exactly ALES60's mono expand of the louder channel) and BOTH channels are scaled by the single fraction
/// `new_key/key`, so the louder-quiet channel lands on `new_key` and the quieter one is scaled by the identical fraction -- the L:R ratio preserved. After the call every written magnitude is at most its input's (reduction,
/// never boost) and no sign is changed. Unit ratio is the identity everywhere. Validates threshold, ratio, and span ONCE before any write -- a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced. Values only -- never length.
pub fn stereo_expand(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
) StereoExpandError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the linked gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the threshold is a legal magnitude -- at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Refused before any write, mirroring ALES60 exactly.
    if (threshold < 1) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (threshold > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    // invariant: the ratio is at least one (num >= den) with a nonzero denominator -- a ratio below one would NARROW the deficit (an upward gesture), a zero denominator would divide by zero. An expander only ever widens.
    if (ratio_den == 0) return error.BadRatio;
    if (ratio_num < ratio_den) return error.BadRatio;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length -- checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never expands one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const thr: i64 = @as(i64, threshold);
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_den);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
        const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
        // The LINKED detector: the louder of the two channels decides for the pair. Below the threshold the whole pair is quiet and is pushed down together; at or above, the pair plays and is left whole.
        const key: i64 = if (ml >= mr) ml else mr;
        if (key < thr and key > 0) {
            const deficit: i64 = thr - key; // strictly positive below the threshold
            // The widened master -- exactly ALES60's mono expand of the louder-quiet channel: widen the deficit by the ratio and push the threshold down by it, floored at silence. divTrunc of positives is floor; the ratio
            // >= 1 makes the widened deficit >= deficit, so new_key <= key, and a wide enough deficit drives new_key to 0 (the gate within).
            const widened: i64 = @divTrunc(deficit * num, den);
            const raw: i64 = thr - widened;
            const new_key: i64 = if (raw < 0) 0 else raw;
            // invariant: the widened master reduces toward silence and never below zero -- the single gain new_key/key is a fraction in [0, 1).
            assert(new_key <= key and new_key >= 0);
            // ONE shared gain across both channels: scale each magnitude by new_key/key. The louder-quiet channel (|x| == key) lands exactly on new_key; the quieter channel lands proportionally, the L:R ratio kept to
            // within one LSB. |x|-new_key <= 32768-32768 fits i64; the quotient is <= |x| since new_key <= key, so it fits i16 and never flips sign.
            const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * new_key, key);
            const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * new_key, key);
            // invariant: the shared gain reduces, never boosts, and never falls below zero, on each channel.
            assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nr <= mr and nr >= 0);
            const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ml <= sample_max
            const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
            sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
            sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
            // invariant: each written sample holds its input's sign (magnitude only shrank), zero being sign-neutral.
            assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
            assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
        }
        // key >= threshold: the pair plays -- both left exactly as they were (continuous at the threshold). key == 0: the pair is already silent -- both already zero, left untouched (silence stays silence, no divide by zero).
    }

    // invariant: an expander changes sample values only -- each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE LINKED DECISION + GAIN LAW -- a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES60's mono expand(left) byte for byte, the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift differing; THE IMAGE LAW -- no
//     magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still out of phase; THE AT-THRESHOLD LAW -- a pair whose linked key reaches the threshold passes byte for byte on both; THE KNOB LAWS -- unit ratio
//     the identity everywhere, a higher ratio expands at least as hard, a huge ratio drives a quiet pair to silence; THE SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- a both-silent pair stays silent, length held, each
//     refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE LINKED DECISION + GAIN LAW -- the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere), thr 1000, ratio 2:1: the louder-quiet left equals ALES60's mono expand(left) byte
    //    for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction new_key/key -- a different, image-preserving result than expanding the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 500, -800, 2000, -300 }; // index 2 is at/above the threshold on the left — that pair plays
        const rr = [_]i16{ 200, -400, 1500, -100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 2, 1);

        // the louder-quiet left equals ALES60's mono expand of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try expand.expand(&mono_l, 0, 4, 1000, 2, 1);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;
        // by hand: 500->0 (deficit500-2=1000, thr−1000=0), 800->600 (deficit200-2=400, 1000−400=600), 2000 plays->2000, 300->0 (deficit700-2=1400, floored).
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, -600, 2000, 0 })) return error.PeakLeftHandWrong;

        // the right scaled by the SAME fraction: idx0 key500 new_key0 -> 0; idx1 key800 new_key600 -> 400-600/800 = 300; idx2 key2000>=thr -> pass 1500; idx3 key300 new_key0 -> 0.
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, -300, 1500, 0 })) return error.LinkedRightWrong;

        // the INDEPENDENT lift genuinely differs -- mono expand of the right alone pushes its own (deeper) deficit, an image-shifting result.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try expand.expand(&mono_r, 0, 4, 1000, 2, 1); // 200→0, 400→0 (deficit600·2=1200 floored), 1500 plays→1500, 100→0
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
        if (!eq(mono_r.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 1500, 0 })) return error.IndependentExpandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE IMAGE LAW -- the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase QUIET pair (right = −left, both below threshold) comes
    //    back STILL out of phase, both scaled by the identical fraction.
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 5000, -400 }; // index 2 is loud (>= thr) — that pair plays on both
        const ir = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -3000, 200 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 3, 1);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }
        if (l[2] != 5000 or r[2] != -3000) return error.LoudPairNotIdentity; // the loud pair passed whole

        // out-of-phase quiet pair: right = −left, both below 1000 -> both scaled by the same fraction, antisymmetry kept.
        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 800, -600, 900, -400 }, &[_]i16{ -800, 600, -900, 400 });
        try stereo_expand(&op, 0, 4, 1000, 2, 1);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
        // by hand 2:1: 800->600 (deficit200-2=400), 600->200 (deficit400-2=800), 900->800 (deficit100-2=200), 400->0 (deficit600-2=1200 floored).
        if (!eq(ol, &[_]i16{ 600, -200, 800, 0 })) return error.OutOfPhaseMagnitudeWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE AT-THRESHOLD LAW -- a master whose every linked key reaches the threshold passes byte for byte on both (the expander is continuous at the threshold).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 5000, -9000, 1000, -1200 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ 3000, -80, 40, -1000 }; // even where the right is quiet (idx1,2) the left is at/above thr, so the pair plays
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 8, 1); // threshold equals the quietest peak — inclusive, still passes
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.AtThresholdNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4a. UNIT RATIO IS THE IDENTITY EVERYWHERE on both -- num == den makes new_key == key, so the fraction is 1 and every below-threshold pair passes byte for byte too.
    {
        const ql = [_]i16{ 300, -800, 20, -900 };
        const qr = [_]i16{ -50, 400, -700, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ql, &qr);
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 3, 3); // unit ratio 3:3
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ql) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &qr)) return error.UnitRatioNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4b. A HIGHER RATIO EXPANDS AT LEAST AS HARD (monotone) and a ratio far past the threshold drives a QUIET pair to SILENCE (the gate within). One quiet pair (both below 1000): 8:1 pushes at least as far down as 2:1,
    //     and a huge ratio drives both to exactly zero.
    {
        const one_l = [_]i16{800};
        const one_r = [_]i16{-500};
        var soft = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&soft, &one_l, &one_r);
        try stereo_expand(&soft, 0, 1, 1000, 2, 1); // 2:1
        var hard = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&hard, &one_l, &one_r);
        try stereo_expand(&hard, 0, 1, 1000, 8, 1); // 8:1
        if (abs16(hard.left.samples()[0]) > abs16(soft.left.samples()[0])) return error.LeftRatioNotMonotone;
        if (abs16(hard.right.samples()[0]) > abs16(soft.right.samples()[0])) return error.RightRatioNotMonotone;
        var shut = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&shut, &one_l, &one_r);
        try stereo_expand(&shut, 0, 1, 1000, 100000, 1); // ratio >> deficit — new_key floored to 0, both to silence
        if (shut.left.samples()[0] != 0 or shut.right.samples()[0] != 0) return error.HighRatioNotSilent;
    }

    // 5a. THE SILENCE LAW -- a both-silent pair stays silent (the key == 0 guard prevents any divide by zero); an all-silent master expands to all zeros.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 4, 1);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;
    }

    // 5b. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; an expander writes values only.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -800, 200, -900, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 300, -700, 500, -50 });
        try stereo_expand(&sc, 0, 5, 1000, 4, 1);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 5c. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a bad threshold, a sub-unity ratio, a zero denominator, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand(&a, 0, 3, 0, 4, 1)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ThresholdRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand(&b, 0, 3, 1000, 1, 2)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.SubUnityRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand(&c, 0, 3, 1000, 4, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.ZeroDenRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand(&d, 1, 3, 1000, 4, 1)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 5d. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to expand.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 500, -500, 900 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_expand(&noop, 1, 0, 1000, 4, 1); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 500, -500, 900 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-expand: the DOWNWARD EXPANDER CARRIED INTO STEREO, the gate's linked DECISION fused with the compressor's linked GAIN — completing the core stereo dynamics quartet. Expand a span of sample-" ++
        "PAIRS in a master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, the detector key per pair being max(|left|, |right|). ALES60 named the mono expander: a sample at or above the threshold passes byte for byte, a sample " ++
        "below has its deficit widened by the ratio and rewritten sign(x)·max(0, threshold − deficit·num/den) — the compressor mirrored under the threshold, deepening the quiet. Carried into stereo it fuses the two " ++
        "linking laws: it links the DECISION like the gate (a pair is expanded iff BOTH channels are below the threshold — expanding one while its partner plays would tear the image) AND the GAIN like the compressor (the " ++
        "widened factor is magnitude-dependent, so one gain from the linked key is applied to both). THE LINKED DECISION + GAIN LAW (the crux): a pair is expanded iff both below the threshold, and where expanded both are " ++
        "scaled by the same fraction new_key/key — the louder-quiet channel equals ALES60's mono expand byte for byte (a left-always-peak master has left' == mono expand(left)), the independent lift genuinely differing. " ++
        "THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE AT-THRESHOLD LAW: a pair whose linked key reaches the " ++
        "threshold passes byte for byte — continuous at the threshold. THE KNOB LAWS: unit ratio the identity everywhere, a higher ratio expands at least as hard (monotone), a ratio far past the threshold drives a quiet " ++
        "pair to silence (the gate within). THE SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a both-silent pair stays silent (the key = 0 guard, no divide by zero), left.len == right.len after, each refusal leaving " ++
        "both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips expanded by one linked gain, one comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide per channel per sample " ++
        "(in i64, no overflow), memoryless, never a length; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
